@RozzieGrandma
If I had found a mentor willing to pay me to do a doctorate in theoretical physics, I would have done that. Some women have found that opportunity but I did not. Partly because I was a non-traditional middle-aged student at the time and the general thinking is that the new ideas come from the young. But elsewhere I've seen it written that the new ideas have historically come from the young--and those new to the field. Which I certainly was. As it turned out, the competition I saw in the graduate physics department did not appeal to me, and I am probably better off psychologically and socially in STEM education research, where cooperation and support are at the core of being of my colleagues.

I remember the frustration of having ideas I wanted to follow, but they have been followed by others and I think now I would not have enjoyed the journey. I am a people person and am where I need to be.

@Andrzej
