@Kelli, Barry's suggestion sounds sensible. With a caveat--when paper solving, I stressed over the last letter before checking the key far more than I stress over my streak. 

Life is stress. When we get to pick which kind, I am happy.

@Andrzej

Though I think Wyra is correct that even logic can't always help on these things, I'm glad I'm with you on this! I don't even notice that number. I see the time when I finish the puzzle and then I just move on to the comments or go back and look at the puzzle again. 

I don't know what number I ever have had as a streak. Thankfully, it's not a motivator for me. My own assessment of my performance is really the only motivator for me.

@Lynn

Interesting idea! I’ve mentioned expansions of the streak concept/criteria myself in the past (in particular, the idea of having an “easy mode” clue set every day which, if selected, would relegate you to a “silver” streak at best), and ultimately I assume such changes would introduce too much complexity for the NYT to seriously consider them…although they do seem sufficiently committed to gamifying the Games space for such things to seem *possible*.

Anyhoo, your platinum tier is interesting. On the one hand, it offers a potential unification between the paper solving medium (i.e. Barry) and the digital. But at the same time……well actually, never mind the qualification I was about to offer — I like it!

@Anonymous
Well, I don't think the programming would add much complexity. Might add some complexity to the display. But the really important thing is, it might trigger a large expansion in the number of requests to "restore my streak."

And as always, the programming would be responsible only for monitoring our clicks. Monitoring "anything equivalent" would be on us.

